facts:
When $m$ is **positive** the line is **increasing**, sloping up from left to right; when $m$ is **negative** it is **decreasing**, sloping down.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.9) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-1.55,-1.55) -- (1.55,1.55);
\node at (-1.1,1.25) {$m>0$};
\node at (0,-2.5) {increasing};
\begin{scope}[shift={(5,0)}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.9) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,red!75!black,<->] (-1.55,1.55) -- (1.55,-1.55);
\node at (1.1,1.25) {$m<0$};
\node at (0,-2.5) {decreasing};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The larger the magnitude of $m$, the **steeper** the line: the sign sets direction, the size sets steepness.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.9) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-0.62,-1.55) -- (0.62,1.55);
\node at (0,-2.5) {larger $|m|$: steeper};
\begin{scope}[shift={(5.6,0)}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.9) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,red!75!black,<->] (-1.55,-0.62) -- (1.55,0.62);
\node at (0,-2.5) {smaller $|m|$: less steep};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two lines with the **same coefficient of $x$** are **parallel**, whatever their constant terms.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-2.4,-1.16) -- (1.6,2.44);
\draw[thick,red!75!black,<->] (-1.6,-2.44) -- (2.4,1.16);
\node[right] at (1.75,2.44) {$y=mx+c$};
\node[right] at (2.55,1.16) {$y=mx+d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two lines with the **same constant term** have the **same $y$-intercept**, crossing the $y$-axis at the same place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-2.2,-0.98) -- (1.8,2.62);
\draw[thick,red!75!black,<->] (-1.8,2.62) -- (2.2,-0.98);
\fill (0,1) circle (2.2pt);
\node[right] at (1.95,2.62) {$y=mx+c$};
\node[right] at (2.35,-0.98) {$y=nx+c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
